From 8b7366c44a183e674c3016f1dcbb7849fe69b514 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: sunrisepeak <speakshen@163.com>
Date: Wed, 7 Oct 2026 10:23:53 +0900
Subject: [clangd] Module cache locks survive dead owners and closed documents

A module cache lock whose owner process died is broken instead of waited on forever, and closing a document cancels the preamble's wait on a module cache lock without deleting a lock a live owner still holds.

* [clangd] Break module cache locks whose owner died

  llvm::LockFileManager detects dead owners on Unix, but its liveness
  check is conservative on Windows: a clangd killed while holding a module
  cache lock leaves every later builder waiting forever.

  After waiting --modules-builder-lock-stale-seconds, a builder now reads
  the owner recorded in the lock file and breaks the lock when that
  process is provably dead. Breaking is safe by design: publishing a BMI
  is an atomic rename and every consumer revalidates, so a broken race
  costs a rebuild, never correctness.

* fix(clangd): document close cancels module cache lock waits
